# The Origin 300 sMDK matrix: one row per processor population, one column
# per standalone diagnostic. A cell is `run` or a reason key from
# lib_prom_smdk.REASONS saying why the population does not run it yet.
# lib_prom_smdk.DIAGS holds each diagnostic's arguments;
# test_prom_smdk_origin300.py and the test_prom_smdk_origin300_<row>.py
# beside it boot the stock PROM and the monitor once per diagnostic.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
#
# These six are the Origin 300 field guide's own set (108-0264-004
# Table 2-1): io8bt, ndir, nmem, scct, System_Level_Test and xbg. The
# package's own diaglist adds two more, sct and CPU_Test, that the guide
# does not call for: sct is dropped for that reason alone, and CPU_Test is
# also dropped because its master compares processor 1's result before
# processor 1 has finished, and why the two processors run at different
# rates on the model is not established. The router, IO7 and GSN programs
# decline for want of hardware the machine does not carry, and iocfg and
# topology are utilities with no verdict, so none of those has a column
# either.
#
# ndir now passes and runs everywhere. nmem now also runs on the pair,
# which needs a longer per-diagnostic timeout than one chassis
# (test_prom_smdk_origin300.py's DIAG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) to sync across the
# two chassis.
#
# budget is the seconds the whole row may take, test start to report;
# every wait is cut to what is left of it. meson.build gives each row's
# target 120s more.
#
# id | cpus | budget | nmem | ndir | scct | System_Level_Test | io8bt | xbg
